---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import MadeByKaj from '../../../components/MadeByKaj.astro';
import LiveCodePlayground from '../../../components/live/LiveCodePlayground.astro';
import CodeBlockWithCopy from '../../../components/live/CodeBlockWithCopy.astro';
import createBrowserHtml from '../../../live-examples/skykit/create-a-browser/index.html?raw';

const basePath = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const installCode = `npm install @found-in-space/skykit@0.2.0-alpha.1 three`;
const importCode = `import { createSkykitBrowser } from '@found-in-space/skykit/browser';

await createSkykitBrowser({
  host: '#viewer',
  status: '#status',
});`;
const tryThisCode = `<div
  data-skykit-browser
  data-skykit-status="#skykit-status"
  data-skykit-magnitude="7"
  data-skykit-speed="4"
  data-skykit-exposure="2600"
  style="width:100%;height:520px;background:#02040b"
></div>

<pre id="skykit-status">Loading stars...</pre>

<script type="module" src="https://esm.sh/@found-in-space/skykit@0.2.0-alpha.1/embed?bundle&deps=three@0.170.0"></script>`;
---

<Layout
	title="Create a SkyKit browser | Found in Space"
	description="Paste an interactive SkyKit star viewer into a static page or CMS block."
>
	<SiteHeader section="Learn & build" />

	<main class="page page--lesson">
		<header class="lesson-hero">
			<nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
				<a href={`${basePath}learn-build/`}>Learn &amp; Build</a>
				<span class="breadcrumb__sep" aria-hidden="true">›</span>
				<a href={`${basePath}learn-build/skykit/`}>SkyKit</a>
				<span class="breadcrumb__sep" aria-hidden="true">›</span>
				<span>Create a browser</span>
			</nav>
			<p class="eyebrow">Lesson 1</p>
			<h1>Create a SkyKit browser</h1>
			<p class="lede">
				Paste an interactive star viewer into a static page or CMS block, then adjust it
				with a few HTML attributes.
			</p>
		</header>

		<article class="lesson-body prose">
			<section>
				<h2>Learning goal</h2>
				<p>
					This first lesson starts with the no-JavaScript embed. You add one viewer
					<code>&lt;div&gt;</code>, one optional status element, and one module script.
				</p>
				<p>
					The viewer already knows how to show stars, respond to drag and keyboard input,
					resize with the page, and write a small loading readout.
				</p>
			</section>
		</article>

		<LiveCodePlayground
			id="skykit-create-a-browser"
			title="Small SkyKit star browser"
			description="Edit the HTML attributes, then run it. The preview opens in a sandboxed iframe."
			initialCode={createBrowserHtml}
			mode="html"
			height="28rem"
		/>

		<article class="lesson-body prose">
			<section class="callout panel">
				<p class="panel__eyebrow">Things to try</p>
				<ul>
					<li>Change the viewer height from <code>500px</code> to <code>700px</code>.</li>
					<li>Add <code>data-skykit-magnitude="7"</code>.</li>
					<li>Add <code>data-skykit-speed="4"</code>.</li>
					<li>Add <code>data-skykit-exposure="2600"</code>.</li>
					<li>Remove the <code>&lt;pre&gt;</code> and <code>data-skykit-status</code> attribute to make a clean decorative embed.</li>
				</ul>
				<CodeBlockWithCopy code={tryThisCode} label="Attribute variant" language="html" />
			</section>

			<section>
				<h2>What changed in the code</h2>
				<p>
					The viewer is marked with <code>data-skykit-browser</code>. The embed script finds
					that element and creates the default SkyKit star viewer there. The status attribute
					is optional; it points at a normal HTML element where the viewer can write a small
					loading/status readout.
				</p>
				<p>
					The important point: the beginner embed owns the plumbing. You do not create a
					renderer, camera, data source, star drawing layer, animation loop, resize handler,
					or cleanup handler in your first SkyKit page.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section class="source-panel panel">
				<p class="panel__eyebrow">Source and next steps</p>
				<h2>Compare this to the package examples.</h2>
				<p>
					This live example is intentionally tiny. After you have a viewer working, the package
					examples are useful when you want diagnostics or a local app structure.
				</p>
				<ul>
					<li><a href="https://github.com/Found-in-Space/skykit" rel="noopener noreferrer">SkyKit repository</a></li>
					<li><a href="https://github.com/Found-in-Space/skykit/tree/main/packages/skykit/examples/free-roam-lesson" rel="noopener noreferrer">Free-roam lesson example</a></li>
					<li><a href={`${basePath}learn-build/skykit/add-an-object/`}>Next: add your own object to space</a></li>
				</ul>
			</section>

			<section class="local-panel panel">
				<h2>Use this locally</h2>
				<p>
					When you move from a pasteable embed to a local app, install SkyKit and Three.js,
					then use the beginner browser entry.
				</p>
				<CodeBlockWithCopy code={installCode} label="Install" language="bash" />
				<CodeBlockWithCopy code={importCode} label="Local imports" language="js" />
			</section>
		</article>

		<MadeByKaj />
	</main>
</Layout>

<style>
	.page--lesson {
		max-width: 76rem;
	}

	.lesson-hero {
		max-width: 60rem;
		padding: 3.5rem 0 1.5rem;
	}

	.lesson-hero h1 {
		margin: 0;
		font-size: 4rem;
		font-weight: 600;
		line-height: 1;
		letter-spacing: 0;
	}

	.lesson-body {
		max-width: 54rem;
	}

	.source-panel {
		margin: 2.5rem 0;
	}

	.source-panel h2 {
		margin-top: 0;
	}

	.local-panel {
		margin: 2.5rem 0;
	}

	.local-panel h2 {
		margin-top: 0;
	}

	@media (max-width: 720px) {
		.lesson-hero h1 {
			font-size: 2.75rem;
		}
	}
</style>
